# .env.example — Copy this file to .env and fill in your real values.
# Do NOT commit .env — it is in .gitignore.
#
# How to get your Discord Webhook URL:
#   Server Settings > Integrations > Webhooks > New Webhook > Copy Webhook URL

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PLACEHOLDER — This is not a real URL. Replace both values before use.
# YOUR_WEBHOOK_ID    → the numeric ID in your Discord webhook URL
# YOUR_WEBHOOK_TOKEN → the token string in your Discord webhook URL
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/YOUR_WEBHOOK_ID/YOUR_WEBHOOK_TOKEN

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional — Slack Incoming Webhook. Every alert goes to EVERY configured
# channel; there are no routing rules and nothing to choose between them.
# That duplication is deliberate: a webhook can be delivered perfectly and
# still not reach you — buried under other notifications, delayed by the
# phone's queue, or silenced by a setting nobody remembers configuring. Two
# independent channels cover the failure no retry policy can.
#
# Leave it unset to run Discord-only; an unconfigured channel is skipped
# silently. If NO channel is configured, every alert is lost and vigil-sre
# says so loudly (metric: alerts_lost_all_channels_total).
#
# How to get it: api.slack.com/apps > your app > Incoming Webhooks >
#   Activate > Add New Webhook to Workspace > pick a channel > Copy
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/YOUR/WEBHOOK/PATH

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional — dead-man's switch. Who watches the watchman?
#
# Every other failure this service can report on itself. This one it cannot:
# if the process dies, the container crash-loops, or the host powers off,
# nobody is left to send the alert — and total silence looks exactly like
# "everything is fine". Point this at an external watchdog that expects a
# ping on a schedule and alerts when one stops arriving (healthchecks.io has
# a free tier and is exactly this).
#
# vigil-sre pings once per COMPLETED run, whether or not targets are down —
# it is watching the monitor, not the targets. Configure the watchdog's
# period to match your run interval (60s by default in docker-compose.yml)
# with a grace period of a few cycles, so one missed ping is not a page.
#
# Leave unset to disable: an unconfigured heartbeat is silent, not an error.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HEARTBEAT_URL=https://hc-ping.com/your-uuid-here

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional — how many days of probe history to keep in history.db before it
# is pruned. Larger values grow the database (roughly 213 bytes/probe/target;
# see README "Latency & BDP Diagnostics" for the measured sizing table).
# Falls back to 30 if unset or not a positive integer.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
HISTORY_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional — expect_substring secrets. targets.yaml can reference any variable
# defined here instead of a literal content-assertion string:
#
#   expect_substring: ${HEALTH_TOKEN}
#
# vigil-sre resolves it from this file at load time and never logs, alerts,
# or persists the real value on a failed check — only "${HEALTH_TOKEN} (from
# env)" is shown. Pick any variable name; there is nothing to configure here
# beyond defining it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HEALTH_TOKEN=your-private-health-check-token
